WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.640
Crop circles, family values, and an&nbsp;
unlikely inspiration led M. Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.439
Shyamalan to leave his mark on the film industry.

00:00:08.439 --> 00:00:14.520
In the days before he began portraying Bruce&nbsp;
Banner in the MCU, Mark Ruffalo was a go-to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:20.100
character actor in indie dramas and other&nbsp;
low-budget challenging projects. However, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.980
before he took on the part of an enormous green&nbsp;
rage monster known as the Hulk, Ruffalo wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:31.920
opposed to appearing in larger-scale features.&nbsp;
After all, he had supporting roles in major genre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.560
movies like "Shutter Island" and "Collateral."&nbsp;
However, these forays into blockbuster territory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:42.780
could have also included taking on the&nbsp;
important part of Merrill Hess in "Signs."

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:48.240
Ruffalo had signed on to portray the role in&nbsp;
the sci-fi thriller, and one wonders what kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.460
of performance he would have delivered working&nbsp;
with Shyamalan. However, this promising actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.980
and filmmaker pairing was simply not meant to be.&nbsp;
Tragedy struck the feature just before shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:05.340
began when Ruffalo had to undergo an operation&nbsp;
to remove a cyst in his ear. While this procedure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:10.080
went off without a hitch, it did ensure that the&nbsp;
actor would be stuck recovering before production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:16.080
began on "Signs." Because of this, Ruffalo had to&nbsp;
drop out of the project. It was a setback for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.020
motion picture, though not a fatal one, as "Signs"&nbsp;
was eventually able to secure Joaquin Phoenix to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:27.000
fill the role instead. While the character of&nbsp;
Merrill Hess still ended up being portrayed by an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.176
acclaimed dramatic performer, it wasn't the actor&nbsp;
Shyamalan originally had in mind for the part.

00:01:32.176 --> 00:01:37.260
Crop circles and the alien-based conspiracy&nbsp;
theories surrounding them have been around for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:43.500
ages. For some filmmakers, the longstanding nature&nbsp;
of this phenomenon might have turned them off from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.540
making this concept the center of a movie. After&nbsp;
all, if mysterious crop circles have been around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:54.840
for so long, what else can you say about them? But&nbsp;
speaking to Blackfilm, Shyamalan noted that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:00.300
universality of crop circles was the exact reason&nbsp;
he wanted to make a movie about them in "Signs."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:05.100
The fact that this whole concept was something&nbsp;
that everyone around the world was aware of made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.420
it seem like a great element to build an&nbsp;
entire globally appealing thriller around.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:15.060
At one point Shyamalan even considered opening&nbsp;
"Signs" in a manner that would have reminded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:20.640
audiences of just how omnipresent the crop&nbsp;
circle phenomenon is. This opening sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.400
would have depicted a family in an unspecified&nbsp;
location in India going outside and discovering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.560
a crop circle. During this sequence, the father&nbsp;
of the family would suddenly become Mel Gibson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.700
and the audience would be thrust into&nbsp;
Philadelphia, where the same bizarre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:40.380
occurrence was happening. Creating visual&nbsp;
parallels between two unconnected families&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.960
would have shown how widespread the crop circles&nbsp;
had become. While this sequence went unrealized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.749
it did epitomize the universality of crop circles&nbsp;
that drew Shyamalan to them in the first place.

00:02:51.749 --> 00:02:55.000
"You see, it was strange&nbsp;
finding the crops that way."

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.900
M. Night Shyamalan's fascination with "Signs"&nbsp;
leading man Mel Gibson stretched back to way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.400
before they ever started collaborating on&nbsp;
this sci-fi thriller. In a UPI interview,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.560
Shyamalan recalled a fond memory of being&nbsp;
transfixed by Gibson's performance the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.060
time he watched "Lethal Weapon." Contrary&nbsp;
to his expectations of what an action movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.320
performance could be, Shyamalan was&nbsp;
blown away by the emotional nuance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.580
and authenticity Gibson brought to the&nbsp;
role. This was not the wooden leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.480
man turn Shyamalan expected from the&nbsp;
genre; this was something special.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.580
As he told UPI:

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:34.740
"This guy did stuff emotionally that had&nbsp;
no business being in an action movie. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:39.000
completely believed the humanity of a man&nbsp;
who was so torn up about losing his wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.400
that he wasn't afraid of dying, which made him&nbsp;
a lethal weapon, and those steps were profound."

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.500
While Shyamalan was floored by Gibson in his&nbsp;
younger years, the actor got to feel the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:55.140
way about the director once he got a chance to&nbsp;
read the screenplay for "Signs." Speaking to BBC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:59.220
Gibson remarked that he often looked&nbsp;
for a handful of important things in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.840
potential acting projects. As soon as he&nbsp;
realized that "Signs" ticked the boxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.120
for everything he was looking for, he quickly&nbsp;
signed on to the project without hesitation.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.560
In an interview with UPI, M. Night&nbsp;
Shyamalan revealed that even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.720
faith is often a prominent part of his movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:21.420
he's not a traditionally religious person.&nbsp;
Rather than be entirely devoted to one religion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:27.300
Shyamalan uses his films to reflect his various&nbsp;
musings and approaches to the very concept of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:33.180
faith. For "Signs," he found that creating a lead&nbsp;
character who awakens to his own potential ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.380
up encapsulating his own thoughts on faith&nbsp;
at the time that he was writing the film.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:43.020
Making a movie about the necessity of clinging&nbsp;
to faith serves as an interesting and hopeful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:47.820
contrast to how the topic has cropped up&nbsp;
in other Shyamalan films. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:53.340
"Unbreakable," which sees the main character's&nbsp;
faith in Mr. Glass shattered once he learns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:58.080
that this man was responsible for the train&nbsp;
crash that revealed his superpowers. Holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.700
faith in someone else only led to bitter&nbsp;
disappointment in "Unbreakable," whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:08.280
faith is what saves the day in "Signs." This&nbsp;
variety and change in how one looks at faith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:13.200
doesn't just ensure that Shyamalan's movies&nbsp;
can stand apart. They also lend fascinating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.272
insight into the man's complicated and&nbsp;
ever-changing relationship with it.

00:05:17.463 --> 00:05:23.820
"There&nbsp;&nbsp; is no one watching out for us,&nbsp;
Merrill. We are all on our own."

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.320
There's no denying that there are&nbsp;
some overlapping similarities between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:33.660
three of M. Night Shyamalan's films. "The&nbsp;
Sixth Sense," "Unbreakable," and "Signs"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.680
all prominently feature children who have&nbsp;
strained relationships with their parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.380
while a spiritual quality exists to a&nbsp;
varying degree across all these movies. Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:49.047
they all show off a style of dialogue that's come&nbsp;
to be known as distinctly the work of Shyamalan.

00:05:49.259 --> 00:05:50.900
"I see dead people."

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.920
However, in the filmmaker's opinion,&nbsp;
"Signs" manages to separate itself from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.580
his earlier works simply by being&nbsp;
a much more emotional creation.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:05.820
Speaking to Blackfilm, Shyamalan was upfront about&nbsp;
how emotional he can be as a person, which informs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:11.760
how much he puts into his work. However, one of&nbsp;
his earlier efforts, "Wide Awake," got pummeled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.980
by critics and moviegoers for its unabashedly&nbsp;
sentimental qualities. While Shyamalan didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:22.380
agree with these critiques, they did inspire him&nbsp;
to pull back on his emotions for his subsequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:28.020
films. Having proven himself as a filmmaker with&nbsp;
"The Sixth Sense" and "Unbreakable," he decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:32.940
it was time to get back to his comfort zone of&nbsp;
writing movies that wear their emotions on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:37.920
sleeves with "Signs." While acknowledging that&nbsp;
being so forward with your movie's emotions can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:43.800
turn off audiences, going this route made "Signs"&nbsp;
something deeply personal to Shyamalan. It ensured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:48.046
the movie could immediately differentiate&nbsp;
itself from his prior filmmaking efforts.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:52.800
"Signs" was a momentous motion picture&nbsp;
in a tragic way that nobody involved in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:56.760
the production of this movie could have&nbsp;
seen coming. The film ended up being one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:01.140
of the first major Hollywood productions to&nbsp;
start shooting after the 9/11 terror attacks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:06.000
Filming on features had briefly ground to a&nbsp;
halt with all the uncertainty looming in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.280
air in the entertainment industry. "Signs" was&nbsp;
the canary in the coal mine to help figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:16.800
what shooting American movies even looked like in&nbsp;
the wake of such unspeakable horrors. It was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.580
experience that Shyamalan still remembers&nbsp;
clearly years after the project wrapped.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:26.760
Speaking to The Ringer in 2020, Shyamalan recalled&nbsp;
that so much of the crew working on "Signs" hailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:32.100
from New York. He felt overwhelmed trying to&nbsp;
lead people at his age in such an unimaginable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:36.660
moment in history. Though the screenplay&nbsp;
for "Signs" was written long before 9/11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:41.100
he felt that the movie ended up being molded by&nbsp;
the public psyche in the wake of the national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.660
tragedy. There was a sense of sorrow and&nbsp;
uncertainty lingering over everybody that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:50.460
helped inform the thriller aspects of "Signs,"&nbsp;
while its emotional moments of family members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:55.080
bonding felt all the more resonant.&nbsp;
9/11 reverberated throughout the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.080 --> 00:08:00.286
even in the way it made history by helping&nbsp;
the industry resume following the tragedy.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.740
Some directors attract notable actors&nbsp;
because of very concrete parts of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:09.000
creative process. In talking about M.&nbsp;
Night Shyamalan to Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.980
"Signs" actor Joaquin Phoenix noted that he&nbsp;
couldn't pinpoint what made the filmmaker so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:19.920
compelling. However, being unable to name that&nbsp;
quality wasn't an issue for Phoenix. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:23.580
the actor actually enjoyed how&nbsp;
undefined Shyamalan was on the set.

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:25.860
Phoenix said the following of the filmmaker:

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.940
"I trusted him, but I can't really&nbsp;
put my finger on why. [...] There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:33.000
was just something in his eyes.&nbsp;
I've thought about this a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:37.320
of times. It's been a year and I can't&nbsp;
say specifically what it is about Night."

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:41.220
Even if the specific qualities that&nbsp;
made the director so fascinating to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:45.720
Phoenix remained unclear, it's obvious&nbsp;
Shyamalan emerged as somebody the actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:49.800
could lean on while making a movie&nbsp;
as intense as "Signs." Sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:54.251
that steadiness is all you need to lure&nbsp;
in a big-name talent to your movie.

00:08:54.329 --> 00:09:00.269
"Listen, there's things I can&nbsp;
take, and a couple things I can't."

00:09:00.578 --> 00:09:04.800
The movies of M. Night Shyamalan have&nbsp;
become famous for their twists. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.320
the best instances of unexpected turns in this&nbsp;
man's work have been ones that function beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.940
their shock value. The twists at the end of&nbsp;
"The Sixth Sense" and "Unbreakable," especially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:20.640
deepen the movie that audiences just watched and&nbsp;
beg for viewers to revisit the entire feature in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:26.460
a new light. In other words, these twists enhance&nbsp;
the characters rather than reduce them to pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:31.860
in an elaborate carnival show. For "Signs,"&nbsp;
Shyamalan attempted to remember this method by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:37.740
crafting a script that could make the major twists&nbsp;
in the climax feel organic instead of contrived.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.600
Speaking to The Ringer, Shyamalan noted that&nbsp;
as a human being, he's always aware of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:48.000
elements of his past, such as a conversation&nbsp;
with his wife, are impacting him in the here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:53.400
and now. For the twists at the end of "Signs,"&nbsp;
he tried to channel this phenomenon by having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:58.620
the means of defeating the alien antagonist&nbsp;
build upon earlier lines of dialogue from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:03.180
younger character and Graham's deceased wife.&nbsp;
What seemed like just ramblings from a dying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:08.580
person or peculiar behavior from a child ended&nbsp;
up meaning much more than that in the long run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:13.560
The twists then become natural extensions of&nbsp;
the behavior of the characters rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:18.420
just an obligatory element in the movie meant&nbsp;
to live up to Shyamalan's pop culture persona.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:23.700
With "The Sixth Sense," M. Night Shyamalan&nbsp;
established himself not just as a filmmaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:29.520
of note, but also as somebody who could attract&nbsp;
moviegoers from all over the planet. Smashing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:35.220
box office with $290 million domestically,&nbsp;
"The Sixth Sense" ended up casting a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.720
shadow over any future movie the director&nbsp;
would make. It would be next to impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:43.800
for nearly any other feature he'd create&nbsp;
to match the gargantuan financial success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:48.780
of this breakthrough title. Though "Signs"&nbsp;
wasn't quite as big as "The Sixth Sense," it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:52.620
still managed to become a very impressive&nbsp;
box office performer in its own right.

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:59.640
In its domestic run, "Signs" grossed over $227&nbsp;
million — a massive amount that put it as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:05.340
biggest movie Mel Gibson has ever acted in by&nbsp;
a considerable margin. Plus, it's the only film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:11.220
he's appeared in on-screen that exceeded the $200&nbsp;
million mark in North America. With an additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:18.960
$180 million internationally, "Signs" grossed&nbsp;
over $408 million globally. It's a significant and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:25.860
profitable haul, considering it was created on a&nbsp;
$70 million budget. The massive success of "Signs"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:31.980
was made evident by where it ended up ranking&nbsp;
among all 2002 titles at the box office. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:37.200
Shyamalan feature was the seventh-biggest movie&nbsp;
of the year worldwide and the sixth biggest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:42.660
the year in North America. It may not have been&nbsp;
quite as big as "The Sixth Sense," but "Signs"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:47.640
still packed a wallop financially. Certainly&nbsp;
everyone involved went smiling to the bank.

